WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.000
I have some ideas to discuss with you. There is a course I give in, I teach law in Israel, and one of the courses I give is called,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:26.000
moresha tisrael which means more or less about the judicial legacy or the legal

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:41.040
tradition yes which was the result of the Tora yes and because I feel that we

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.040
We mistake ourselves when we think of Judaism as a religion.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.120
Religion has to do with rituals.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.220
And of course we do have rituals,

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.220
tefillin, talet, candle sabbat, mezuzot, kasher, tareb,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.780
but Judaism is much more than a ritual.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.600
It's a whole system.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.020
And I just want to mention one point

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.900
in which is a total revolution.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:15.900
revolution that we invented and we promoted something which changed the face of civilization.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:27.900
A Jewish writer who wrote in Greek, the second century BCE, you know, in Egypt, he wrote

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:34.900
a history of the Jews. We don't have it. We have only some paragraphs, quotations of people.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:02:02.560
He says something very interesting. He says, Moses wrote the first book. The first book in humanity was written by Moses. Of course, many historians say, well, no, you know, I mean, we find the inscriptions in the Egyptian temples, I don't know, Babylonian monuments, cuneiform clays.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:23.380
So what do you mean that, you know, it's the first book? And all these stories I'm aware of, they dismiss him as being just a pompous Jew. But I think he's right. Because a book is something much more than writing.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:39.680
Let me begin just by pointing one difference. If an inscription, you have to go to the inscription. You have to go to the temple. When you're in the temple, you see the inscription and somebody tells you what it means.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.760
the Jews invented a book

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.440
a book

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.600
you don't go to the book

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.080
you take the book with you

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.260
I think it's a very important difference

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.680
at the temple

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.740
the inscriptions

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.660
a priest will tell you what it means

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.260
in a book

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.600
you read it

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.600
in your own privacy

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:15.080
There is a one-on-one relationship between you and the author of the book.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:22.600
And all of a sudden, because you are in touch with the book, you transform.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.600
You have ideas.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:31.540
A human with a book is much powerful than a human with a tank.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:37.060
Because this person already thinks at a different level.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.660
So, we invented the book.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:49.920
And in the book, one of the most important aspects of the book is the idea of law.

